By the change-of-base formula,
\[
\frac{\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})}
=\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-4}\cdot\frac{\ln(k+1)}{\ln k}
=\frac{k-1}{k-2}\cdot\frac{k+1}{k+2}\cdot\frac{\ln(k+1)}{\ln k}.
\]
Thus the product splits into three telescoping products:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\prod_{k=4}^{63}\frac{k-1}{k-2}
&=\frac32\cdot\frac43\cdots\frac{62}{61}
=\frac{62}{2}=31,\\
\prod_{k=4}^{63}\frac{k+1}{k+2}
&=\frac56\cdot\frac67\cdots\frac{64}{65}
=\frac5{65}=\frac1{13},\\
\prod_{k=4}^{63}\frac{\ln(k+1)}{\ln k}
&=\frac{\ln64}{\ln4}=3.
\end{aligned}
\]
Therefore the given product equals
\[
31\cdot\frac1{13}\cdot3=\frac{93}{13}.
\]
Since \(93=3\cdot31\) is relatively prime to \(13\), we obtain \(m+n=93+13\), so the answer is
\[
\boxed{106}.
\]
